\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {5}{\ignorespaces First plot: Radial density profiles through simulation time. The x-axis begins at $\sim 1\,\mathrm  {AU}$, corresponding to the resolution limit of the simulation. The fit for the region outside of the disk is consistently $\rho \propto r^{-1.7}$ accross all times. \\ Second plot: Gas mass in a ball vs radius of the ball before the formation of the first sink. The fit is roughly $M \propto r^1.3$.\\ \added {Third plot: rate of change of the gas mass enclosed within a sphere of radius $r$, i.e.\ the integrated ``mass of the ball'' accretion rate $\dot  {M}(<r) = \mathrm  {d}M(<r)/\mathrm  {d}t$. At later times the inner region gains mass slower than at the eariler times (mostly close to the time of the first sink forming). There is a characteristic "flattening" at smaller radii inside of the disk before the first star has formed. Outer shells gain mass faster at all times -- a characteristic of "outside-in" collapse.} Smallest shells have a drop in $\dot  {M}(<r)$, associated with coming close to the resolution limit.\\ Bottom plot: evolution of radial profile of kinematic virial parameter with time. The disk starts clearly sub-virial in the region inside of the disk ($ < 10^{-2} $ pc), with virial parameter increasing by orders of magnitude at later times. \added {Note that this radial profile spans several orders of magnitude, whereas the global (mass-weighted) virial ratio in \autoref {fig:energy_evol} settles at $\sim 1$: the two probe different scales — the disk interior here versus the whole disk there.}}}{8}{figure.5}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{loc}{\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{Third plot: rate of change of the gas mass enclosed within a sphere of radius $r$, i.e.\ the integrated ``mass of the ball'' accretion rate $\dot  {M}(<r) = \mathrm  {d}M(<r)/\mathrm  {d}t$. At later times the inner region gains mass slower than at the eariler times (mostly close to the time of the first sink forming). There is a characteristic "flattening" at smaller radii inside of the disk before the first star has formed. Outer shells gain mass faster at all times -- a characteristic of "outside-in" collapse.}}{8}{section*.7}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{loc}{\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{Note that this radial profile spans several orders of magnitude, whereas the global (mass-weighted) virial ratio in \autoref {fig:energy_evol} settles at $\sim 1$: the two probe different scales — the disk interior here versus the whole disk there.}}{8}{section*.8}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:shell_mass_accretion}{{5}{8}{First plot: Radial density profiles through simulation time. The x-axis begins at $\sim 1\,\mathrm {AU}$, corresponding to the resolution limit of the simulation. The fit for the region outside of the disk is consistently $\rho \propto r^{-1.7}$ accross all times. \\ Second plot: Gas mass in a ball vs radius of the ball before the formation of the first sink. The fit is roughly $M \propto r^1.3$.\\ \added {Third plot: rate of change of the gas mass enclosed within a sphere of radius $r$, i.e.\ the integrated ``mass of the ball'' accretion rate $\dot {M}(<r) = \mathrm {d}M(<r)/\mathrm {d}t$. At later times the inner region gains mass slower than at the eariler times (mostly close to the time of the first sink forming). There is a characteristic "flattening" at smaller radii inside of the disk before the first star has formed. Outer shells gain mass faster at all times -- a characteristic of "outside-in" collapse.} Smallest shells have a drop in $\dot {M}(<r)$, associated with coming close to the resolution limit.\\ Bottom plot: evolution of radial profile of kinematic virial parameter with time. The disk starts clearly sub-virial in the region inside of the disk ($ < 10^{-2} $ pc), with virial parameter increasing by orders of magnitude at later times. \added {Note that this radial profile spans several orders of magnitude, whereas the global (mass-weighted) virial ratio in \autoref {fig:energy_evol} settles at $\sim 1$: the two probe different scales — the disk interior here versus the whole disk there.}}{section*.8}{}}
