\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {7}{\ignorespaces  Radial profiles of inward radial velocity $-v_r$, azimuthal velocity $v_{\phi }$, sound speed $c_s$, magnitude of turbulent velocity $\left | \delta v \right |$, radial/azimuthal/vertical turbulent velocity dispersion $\sigma _r/\sigma _\phi /\sigma _z$, Keplerian speed $v_K$ of gas particles in the disks. Bright lines show functions with spline smoothing, dim lines show actual data. $\sigma _r$ grows with time, as well as $\left | \delta v \right |$. Radial infall $(v_r)$ speeds up until the bulk star formation events ($1.3-3.6\,\mathrm  {kyr}$), and slows down afterwards. Disk rotation $(v_{\phi })$ is constantly speeding up, leveling off outside of the disk ($r \gtrsim 2500$ AU). $\sigma _r, \sigma _{\phi }, \sigma _z$ track each other very closely, but $\sigma _z$ decouples from the other two by the $\Delta t \gtrsim 9$ kyr time outside of the disk. Turbulence is supersonic at all times, tracking Keplerian speed $v_K$ closely for most times outside of the disk, but falling below it inside of the disk.}}{10}{figure.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:kinematics_wide}{{7}{10}{Radial profiles of inward radial velocity $-v_r$, azimuthal velocity $v_{\phi }$, sound speed $c_s$, magnitude of turbulent velocity $\left | \delta v \right |$, radial/azimuthal/vertical turbulent velocity dispersion $\sigma _r/\sigma _\phi /\sigma _z$, Keplerian speed $v_K$ of gas particles in the disks. Bright lines show functions with spline smoothing, dim lines show actual data. $\sigma _r$ grows with time, as well as $\left | \delta v \right |$. Radial infall $(v_r)$ speeds up until the bulk star formation events ($1.3-3.6\kyr $), and slows down afterwards. Disk rotation $(v_{\phi })$ is constantly speeding up, leveling off outside of the disk ($r \gtrsim 2500$ AU). $\sigma _r, \sigma _{\phi }, \sigma _z$ track each other very closely, but $\sigma _z$ decouples from the other two by the $\Delta t \gtrsim 9$ kyr time outside of the disk. Turbulence is supersonic at all times, tracking Keplerian speed $v_K$ closely for most times outside of the disk, but falling below it inside of the disk}{figure.7}{}}
\@writefile{loc}{\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{The turbulent velocity dispersion increases inward, tracking the increase in gravitational potential. Because the collapse compresses the gas faster than the turbulence can decay, the turbulence is continuously re-driven by the contraction itself rather than by stellar feedback \citep  {robertson_goldreich12}; feedback only begins to contribute once the first protostar forms.}}{10}{section*.9}\protected@file@percent }
