\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {9}{\ignorespaces Top: Face-on maps of the local Toomre $Q$ parameter at six epochs. The black contour marks $Q = 1$. \\ Bottom: Evolution of radial profile of Toomre $Q$ (heatmap), and sink formations, mergers, and radial positions' evolution (lines). \\ At early times the gas is globally unstable ($Q < 1$); at late times the inner disk is strongly stabilised while the outer spiral arms remain near or below $Q = 1$. Radial profile of Toomre $Q$ at six epochs. The horizontal dashed line marks $Q = 1$. At early times the entire region has $Q < 1$; at late times a radial gradient develops, with $Q > 10^3$ at $r < 100\,\mathrm  {AU}$ falling to $Q \sim 1$ at $r \sim 2000\,\mathrm  {AU}$. {\textcolor {darkgreen}{[SK: I would increase the size of the stars a little bit and make them a different color so it's easy to make out. Maybe in the bottom panel, color the formation time of the sink to be the same as the color of the line? Maybe even make some of the lines thicker according to the mass of the star. It looks like some of the lines are well correlated with the Toomre-Q evolution -- I wonder if the more massive stars are responsible for this.]}} }}{12}{figure.9}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:toomre_Q_merged}{{9}{12}{Top: Face-on maps of the local Toomre $Q$ parameter at six epochs. The black contour marks $Q = 1$. \\ Bottom: Evolution of radial profile of Toomre $Q$ (heatmap), and sink formations, mergers, and radial positions' evolution (lines). \\ At early times the gas is globally unstable ($Q < 1$); at late times the inner disk is strongly stabilised while the outer spiral arms remain near or below $Q = 1$. Radial profile of Toomre $Q$ at six epochs. The horizontal dashed line marks $Q = 1$. At early times the entire region has $Q < 1$; at late times a radial gradient develops, with $Q > 10^3$ at $r < 100\AU $ falling to $Q \sim 1$ at $r \sim 2000\AU $. \skcomment {I would increase the size of the stars a little bit and make them a different color so it's easy to make out. Maybe in the bottom panel, color the formation time of the sink to be the same as the color of the line? Maybe even make some of the lines thicker according to the mass of the star. It looks like some of the lines are well correlated with the Toomre-Q evolution -- I wonder if the more massive stars are responsible for this.}}{figure.9}{}}
\citation{STARFORGE_Towa_Grudic_2021}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {10}{\ignorespaces Toomre Q radial profiles time evolution. The disk starts with $Q < 1$ inside and $Q \approx 1$ near the edge, but $Q$ rises with disk evolution, since gas is removed from the disk because of star formation events. Center of the disk is extremely stable ($Q \gtrsim 10^2$) by the 15 kyr mark, preventing any further star formation in the middle. {\textcolor {darkgreen}{[SK: I don't think this plot adds any new information, you can get rid of it to keep things more concise.]}}}}{13}{figure.10}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:profile_toomre_Q}{{10}{13}{Toomre Q radial profiles time evolution. The disk starts with $Q < 1$ inside and $Q \approx 1$ near the edge, but $Q$ rises with disk evolution, since gas is removed from the disk because of star formation events. Center of the disk is extremely stable ($Q \gtrsim 10^2$) by the 15 kyr mark, preventing any further star formation in the middle. \skcomment {I don't think this plot adds any new information, you can get rid of it to keep things more concise.}}{figure.10}{}}
