\@writefile{loc}{\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{A key qualitative difference from these earlier studies is that our protostars form within a single, large-scale disk rather than in small, circum-protostellar disks. The disk extends to $\sim 2500\,\,\mathrm  {AU}$ and fragments repeatedly, so the entire protostellar population shares a common gas reservoir and competes for the same infalling material. This geometry naturally accounts for the large number of sinks and for the accelerating, super-quadratic stellar-mass growth we report.}}{19}{section*.14}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {subsection}{\numberline {4.4}Star formation efficiency}{19}{subsection.4.4}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{loc}{\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{Jets are not present in the current run: a bug in the launch implementation suppresses them (see \autoref {sec:methods}), so we cannot assess their impact on the star formation efficiency here. Once jets are enabled, they are expected to become progressively more important as individual protostars contract and their surface escape velocities increase, potentially quenching accretion and reducing the SFE from its current high value \citep  {STARFORGE_Towa_Grudic_2021, meziani26}.}}{19}{section*.15}\protected@file@percent }
